# OzzBooks — Addendum: Playlists for ebooks & comics

This addendum extends `Claude.md` with a planned (not yet built) fix:
**Playlists (the "Add to Playlist" / "Up Next" feature) currently only
works for audiobooks.** Keep this alongside `Claude.md` and the other
addenda so future sessions have this context.

**Status: planned, not started.** Surfaced 2026-09-02 while scoping the
comics Book Detail page (`Ozzbooks_Addendum_Comics`) — Jim's expectation
was that this already worked for ebooks, which turned out not to be
true (`BookDetail.tsx` hides `AddToPlaylist` behind `book.format !==
'epub'` today). This is being tracked separately from the comics work
because it's a pre-existing ebook gap, not something new comics
introduced — comics shouldn't ship with the same gap a second time.

## What "Playlists" actually does today (why this isn't just deleting a guard)

Two kinds of playlist exist, and they have very different relationships
to auto-advance:

- **Named/custom playlists** (created via `Playlists.tsx`) are purely
  organizational — add, reorder, remove, browse (`PlaylistDetail.tsx`).
  Nothing in the app currently treats "finished this book" as a signal
  to jump to the next item in one of these; you just come back and pick
  your next read/listen manually. **This half is already completely
  format-agnostic** — nothing about add/reorder/remove/browse assumes
  audio.
- **"Up Next"** is a single reserved playlist (`is_reserved`) with real
  auto-advance behavior, and that behavior is audio-specific under the
  hood: `NowPlaying.tsx`'s effect watches `player.finished`, which
  `PlayerContext.tsx` derives from the `<audio>` element's native
  `ended` event. When a book finishes playing *and* it was the head of
  Up Next, the effect pops it and auto-loads + auto-plays the next
  item. **Ebooks and comics have no native "ended" event** — there's no
  browser-level signal for "the reader closed the last page" the way
  there is for "the audio file stopped."

So this isn't one gap, it's two different-sized ones layered together.

## Recommended split

**1. Ship immediately — low risk, fixes the actual reported gap.**
Remove the `book.format !== 'epub'` guard on `AddToPlaylist` in
`BookDetail.tsx`, and extend the same check to exclude comics too once
`ComicReader` exists (or rather, extend it to *include* them — the
guard becomes "hide only if there's truly nothing to queue," which
never applies to a real book). Named playlists and adding-to-Up-Next
both work immediately for ebooks and comics exactly as they do for
audio today, since neither depends on the `ended`-event machinery —
only *advancing out of* Up Next automatically does. This alone fixes
what Jim already expected to work for ebooks, and comics get it from
day one instead of needing a second follow-up fix later.

**2. Real auto-advance needs a "finished" signal invented for each
reader, since none exists natively.** Define it explicitly:
- **Ebook**: reached (or passed) the CFI corresponding to the end of
  the last chapter — the same "last chapter reached" heuristic
  `Library.tsx`'s `bookStatus()` already uses to bucket a book as
  "finished" for the Status filter, just evaluated live inside
  `EbookReader.tsx` as it happens, instead of read back from stored
  progress after the fact.
- **Comic**: current page index reaches `page_count - 1` (or the
  reader advances past it from the last page).

**3. Don't silently auto-navigate on finish, unlike audio — this is a
deliberate difference, not a shortcut.** Audio's silent auto-advance
fits passive listening: nobody's watching the screen when one file
rolls into the next, so silently starting the next one is the whole
point. Reading is active — yanking someone straight into a new ebook or
comic the instant they tap past the last page would be jarring, not
convenient, and risks feeling like a bug ("why did the screen just
change on me?"). Instead, both readers should show an explicit prompt
at the end-of-book/issue screen when the just-finished item was the
head of Up Next — something like **"Up Next: [cover] — Continue to
[title]?"** — a deliberate tap, not an automatic transition.

**4. Share the advance logic instead of duplicating it a third time.**
The fetch-Up-Next / check-if-head-matches / pop-the-consumed-item /
look-up-the-next-item sequence currently lives inline in
`NowPlaying.tsx`, coupled to `player.finished`. Pull it into a small
shared hook (e.g. `useUpNextAdvance(book, finishedSignal)`) that all
three surfaces — `NowPlaying.tsx`, `EbookReader.tsx`, `ComicReader.tsx`
— call with their own definition of "finished," rather than copying the
fetch/pop/lookup sequence three times or leaving it stranded inside a
component only the audio player renders.

## Open questions to settle before implementing (not decided here)

- Where does the "Continue to [title]?" prompt actually render for
  ebooks/comics — an overlay on the last page itself, or a dedicated
  "you finished this" screen replacing the reader chrome? Needs a
  concrete mock, not decided here.
- Should partial progress ever get treated as "close enough to
  finished" to surface the Up Next prompt early (e.g. last page of an
  epilogue that's mostly blank)? No evidence this is a real problem —
  don't build a fuzzy threshold speculatively; exact "reached the end"
  is fine to start.
